# What `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile is allowed to see.
#
# Without this file the build context is the whole working tree. Measured on a developer machine:
# **53 GB**, and 39 minutes of transferring before the builder compiled a single line — because the
# context swept in `target/` (41 GB of build output) and `.claude/worktrees/` (agent checkouts,
# each with a `target/` of its own). It then filled the disk. None of it is used: the builder stage
# runs `cargo build`, which regenerates everything it needs from source.
#
# It matters on CI too, where there is no `target/` but `.git`, `docs/book/` and the tier-3
# benchmark corpora would still be copied into a layer for nothing.
#
# This list excludes, so anything the build genuinely needs must simply not appear here. Keep it in
# step with the `COPY` lines in `Dockerfile` — an over-broad entry here surfaces as a confusing
# "file not found" inside the builder, not as a warning.

# Build output: regenerated by `cargo build`, and the single largest thing in the tree.
target/
**/target/

# Coding-agent worktrees. Each is a full checkout plus its own `target/`; see
# `scripts/reclaim-agent-space.sh`, which exists because these once reached 98 GB.
.claude/

# History. The build needs the source, not how it got there, and copying `.git` into a layer is one
# of the ways repository history ends up published inside an image.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# CI, tooling and editor configuration. None of it is read by `cargo build`.
.github/
.vscode/
.idea/
.pre-commit-config.yaml
.yamllint.yaml
.shellcheckrc
taplo.toml
clippy.toml
deny.toml
cliff.toml
_typos.toml

# Documentation and benchmark assets. `docs/book/` and the tier-3 corpora are large and play no
# part in producing the binary.
docs/
bench/
scripts/
justfile
*.md

# Container definitions, including this image's own and the Java CDM benchmark image.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
